            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. The majority of the air leakage was observed around the windows and doors of the home. The weather stripping along all of the doors and windows should be replaced. 

Air infiltration was also observed around the frame of the windows and doors. I recommend you remove the trim and seal the framing members supporting the windows and doors. This can be done with either low expansion spray foam or interior grade caulking. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 20% improvement you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 450&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;19.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 600 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.

I think for your home, you could easily achieve a better result than 20%, increasing your yearly savings. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a home energy evaluation, which includes an air leakage test, before and after your retrofits. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 studs with 6 inches (R20) of fiberglass batt insulation. In conjunction with a future upgrade to the exterior siding of this house, I have modeled adding another R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation to the exterior of all walls, including the additions.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By adding R15 of semi-rigid, breathable exterior insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 490&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;21.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,875 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</mainWalls>
                <windows>

As all of your windows are older wood framed ones (with cracked insulators between the panes), I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading all of them with ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing all 13 windows with triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged, ENERGY STARÂ® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 100&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;4.3 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



Please note, the above savings do not reflect the air sealing aspect of replacing windows and properly sealing the framing. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</windows>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank, that is indirectly heated by your boiler as well. If the boiler is replaced with a more efficienct model, this will also increase the efficiency of your hot water heating. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your &lt;b&gt;upgraded boiler&lt;/b&gt; to heat your hot water, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save an additional $ 40&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;1.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

&lt;b&gt;Boiler&lt;/b&gt;



Your current oil fired boiler was originally rated at 80% AFUE. When it is time to replace it, I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified high-efficiency oil boiler with a minimum efficiency of 87% AFUE. If you choose a high-efficiency oil furnace (direct vented out a side wall), the current chimney can be eliminated altogether.









&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a sealed combustion, oil-fired boiler with an efficiency of 87% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 480&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;20.4 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Domestic Hot Water&lt;/b&gt;



You currently have a 184 litre electric hot water tank, that is indirectly heated by your boiler as well. If the boiler is replaced with a more efficienct model, this will also increase the efficiency of your hot water heating. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your &lt;b&gt;upgraded boiler&lt;/b&gt; to heat your hot water, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save an additional $ 40&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and decrease your annual energy usage by an additional &lt;b&gt;1.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Woodstove&lt;/b&gt;



If you decide to install a woodstove, I recommend installing an EPA-certified wood-burning appliance. Ensure that the installation complies with local building, fire codes, and includes a dedicated combustion air duct. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing an EPA-certified woodstove and using it for 50% of your heat load, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 250&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

&lt;b&gt;Note:&lt;/b&gt; If you harvest your own wood, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,350&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>

Harnessing solar energy in the NWT may seem unlikely. The short and dark days of winter provide little or no usable energy, depending on the latitude in the NWT. However, the long hours of sunlight in spring and summer can provide usable heat and electricity for up to eight months of the year.  As prices continue to drop, solar PV panels are becoming competitive with the cost of diesel generation. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By installing a 3 kW solar photovoltaic system, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,290&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;14.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Alternative Energy Technologies Program rebate, you must apply and receive pre-approval. The grant amount differs according to the purchase cost of the system, 1/3 of purchase cost, up to a maximum of $2,500 per year. Applicants are encouraged to contact the AEA for pre-approval prior to installation. Projects will be funded on a first come, first serve basis until the program is fully subscribed. Projects must be installed and operational to receive the rebate. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/alternative-energy-technologies-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</Renewables>
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.30 per kWh

Oil  - $0.84 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord 

Pellets - $6.58 per bag



